-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Name a selection bias, one access control, and a cautious recommendation for the fictional staffing analysis.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Healthcare findings should fit the evidence, protect patients, and support qualified decisions.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Stock market analysis, credit risk modeling, and fraud detection.' WHERE id = 'dc82cfed-5b89-41cc-ae25-460c758aa937' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Evaluate financial predictions with time-aware splits, calibrated risk, and clear limits on who may act.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A lender tests a default-risk model and randomly mixes future repayment records into training data.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would that test reflect the information available when a real loan decision is made?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Financial data changes over time and labels may arrive late. Use temporal validation, check calibration and subgroup error, document feature availability, and follow applicable governance.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Train on earlier applications and evaluate on a later period. Compare predicted risk with observed outcomes by score band and relevant population.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Identify one leakage path, one useful calibration check, and one human or policy review needed before a score affects applicants.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Risk scores need time-valid evidence, review, and an explicit decision boundary.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Analyzing biological data, especially genetic sequences and molecular structures.' WHERE id = 'c8610c81-2add-4716-942f-cec61bee8045' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Recognize the data, validation, and privacy constraints in a biological analysis without overinterpreting a pattern.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A research group compares gene-expression measurements from a small cohort to identify candidates for further study.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Could batch effects or repeated measures explain an apparent difference?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Biological datasets often have many features, small samples, complex measurement pipelines, and sensitive provenance. Multiple testing and population limits can make apparent patterns unstable.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Check sample metadata, normalize using an appropriate documented method, account for batch and participant structure, and label findings exploratory until independently validated.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List one batch effect, one privacy safeguard, and one independent validation step for a fictional gene-expression result.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A computational signal can prioritize research; it does not establish a medical conclusion on its own.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Using data-driven insights to support business decision-making processes.' WHERE id = 'f5881786-f492-4274-a54a-9ef3b19dcff3' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Translate analysis into a decision-ready finding with a defined metric and an accountable next action.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A retailer reports that revenue rose, but managers cannot tell whether higher prices, more orders, or one seasonal product drove the change.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What comparison would help the team choose an action?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A business metric needs a definition, population, time window, and owner. Break a total into meaningful components and separate observed change from explanation.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Compare order count, average order value, and product mix by week, then recommend a small promotion test with a follow-up measure.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Write a finding with metric, period, comparison, caveat, and next step. What additional data would distinguish seasonality from a promotion effect?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A recommendation is strongest when its metric and next decision are explicit.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Apache Kafka, Apache Storm, and Apache Flink for real-time analytics.' WHERE id = '8fad55c1-ae15-4404-a574-14abc35e8a86' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Distinguish event streaming from scheduled batch work and define freshness, ordering, and recovery needs.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A service wants to notify a support team when a queue has been blocked for several minutes.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Does the alert need every event immediately, or a periodic summary with lower cost?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Streaming systems process events continuously and must account for late, duplicate, and out-of-order messages. Delivery guarantees and idempotent processing shape correctness.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Aggregate queue events in short windows, use event timestamps, deduplicate by stable event ID, and alert only after a threshold persists.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose a freshness target and duplicate-handling rule for a live service alert. When would a daily batch be the better choice?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Real-time adds operational complexity; use it when the decision benefits from fresh data.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Document, key-value, wide-column, and graph databases.' WHERE id = '8fd209d1-65ff-41af-af84-589199ed4a7d' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Match a NoSQL data model to access patterns and explain consistency and duplication trade-offs.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A mobile app must read a user’s recent activity quickly, while analysts occasionally ask questions across all users.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Can one optimized schema serve both request patterns equally well?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Key-value, document, wide-column, and graph databases organize data differently. Denormalization can improve known reads but creates update consistency and duplication work.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Store recent activity by user and time for the application path; send curated events to a separate analytical store rather than making one database answer every question.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose a primary access pattern for a document store and name a query that would fit poorly. What consistency issue can duplicated fields create?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Choose the data model from real reads and writes, and make trade-offs explicit.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'In-memory computing, SQL, streaming, machine learning, and graph processing capabilities.' WHERE id = 'ade23515-20ac-408b-8099-fbef5f6ba144' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Describe how distributed data transformations and partitioning affect a Spark job’s performance and cost.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A daily analytics job repeatedly shuffles a large table because it groups records by a key that is badly distributed.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What work moves across the cluster, and could skew put most data on one worker?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Spark executes transformations across partitions and may shuffle data between workers. Caching helps reused data, but excessive caching and poorly sized partitions consume resources.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Inspect the execution plan, filter early, select needed columns, and measure a representative run before tuning executor settings.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Find one operation likely to cause a shuffle in a group-by pipeline. Name one metric to compare before and after a change.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Distributed code still needs profiling; parallelism cannot fix a poor query plan for free.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'HDFS for storage and MapReduce for processing, plus related projects like Hive and Pig.' WHERE id = '816bc31a-b529-4bea-9dd8-b855b254a976' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Explain distributed file storage and batch processing conceptually and identify when this legacy ecosystem is relevant.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>An archive team processes a very large, mostly static collection of logs overnight.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would a batch job suit a result needed in seconds for an interactive user?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>HDFS distributes large files across a cluster; MapReduce runs batch transformations near stored data. Hadoop-related tools support particular workloads but may carry substantial operational overhead.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>A batch task can count event types by partition, combine partial counts, and write aggregate results. Compare this with managed cloud analytics before operating a cluster.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose batch or interactive processing for a nightly archive report and a live fraud decision. State one reason the Hadoop stack may be unnecessary.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Learn the distributed processing idea and select a current tool only when requirements justify it.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Volume, velocity, variety, veracity, and value - the 5 V''s of big data.' WHERE id = 'be1491bf-5599-4010-83d4-830d6cc04925' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Decide whether scale, speed, or data variety actually requires distributed processing.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A local nonprofit has a few thousand donation rows but is considering a large distributed platform after hearing about big data.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which measurable constraint does the current spreadsheet or database fail to meet?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Volume, velocity, variety, and reliability describe different data challenges. Distributed systems add coordination, network, and operational costs; they are not automatically better for small data.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Profile dataset size, refresh rate, and query duration first. A well-indexed relational database may be simpler and cheaper for a modest reporting workload.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Name the bottleneck that could justify distributed processing and one sign that the simpler current system remains sufficient.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Use the smallest architecture that meets measured needs and leaves room to grow.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Serialization, containerization, API serving, and performance monitoring in production.' WHERE id = '3f1125d1-5a06-433d-823f-7a5fbbb877f3' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Describe the data, model, interface, monitoring, and rollback pieces needed for a bounded deployment.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A model predicts stock shortages in a notebook, but planners update inventory in a separate system each morning.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Where will the prediction appear, and what happens when data is missing or stale?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Deployment connects a versioned artifact to a repeatable input contract and an owned workflow. Monitoring covers service health, data quality, drift, and delayed outcomes.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Publish a daily batch with item ID, forecast horizon, model version, and confidence range. Alert on missing inputs and allow planners to return to the existing process.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Draw a small prediction flow and mark one validation check, one health signal, one owner, and one rollback trigger.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A model is operational only when people can use it safely and the team can detect when it stops helping.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Domain knowledge, transformation, creation, and selection of relevant features.' WHERE id = 'aa79f5f0-c622-4843-b93e-338a08a7730e' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Create features that reflect the prediction moment and test whether they add signal without leakage.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A maintenance model predicts failures, and a technician proposes using the last repair date and final inspection outcome.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Was the inspection outcome known when the prediction would be made?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Feature engineering transforms raw fields into useful signals. Selection should consider availability, leakage, stability, privacy, and whether a feature adds value beyond a baseline.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Compute days since last completed repair from records available before scoring. Fit scaling and feature selection within each training fold.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Classify three candidate fields as available, leakage-prone, or sensitive for a prediction made at 8 am each day. What documentation should accompany a derived feature?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A predictive feature must be available at the time of use and defensible for the decision.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Train-test split, cross-validation, and metrics for regression and classification.' WHERE id = '29d23554-4adb-4f0d-83b3-2debb2a865b5' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Design a validation split that represents future use and select metrics tied to the decision’s error costs.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A fraud model is evaluated by randomly splitting individual transactions, even though many transactions belong to the same accounts and time periods.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Could related transactions appear on both sides of the split and make the result look easier?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Holdout and cross-validation estimate generalization only when partitions respect time, people, sites, or other dependencies. Select metrics such as precision, recall, calibration, or MAE based on the decision.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Split transactions by time and account group; report recall at an alert workload and inspect performance for new accounts separately.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose a split and primary metric for predicting next-month demand. Explain one consequence of using a random row split.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Evaluation is a design decision about which future cases the reported score represents.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'K-means, hierarchical clustering, PCA, t-SNE, and association rule learning.' WHERE id = 'b9e40347-a362-4fa3-8100-4d04a44b22f0' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Use clustering or dimension reduction to explore structure while avoiding claims that discovered groups are inherently meaningful.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A museum groups visitor sessions to improve exhibit guidance but has no labels for visitor intent.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would clusters reveal genuine needs, or simply differences in visit length and device type?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Clustering groups observations by a chosen distance; PCA compresses correlated numeric variation. Results depend on scaling, features, and parameters and require domain interpretation.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Scale appropriate numeric session features, cluster a training sample, then profile groups and test stability across time before proposing a different visitor experience.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Name a feature that could dominate distance unfairly and a check to see whether the clusters remain stable under a reasonable change.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Unsupervised output is a hypothesis about structure, not a discovered ground-truth category.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Linear regression, logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, and support vector machines.' WHERE id = 'bc7edd5d-e1d9-4358-80ec-e043e20d0ce0' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose a supervised model from target type, data size, interpretability, and baseline performance.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A library predicts whether a requested book will be returned late using only information available when it is checked out.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Is the target a number of days or a yes/no event, and what simple prediction should be tested first?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Regression predicts numeric outcomes; classification predicts categories. Linear models, trees, and ensembles trade off assumptions, flexibility, interpretability, and compute.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Compare a majority-class baseline and a small tree for late-return classification. Exclude post-return events because they would leak the outcome.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Pick a baseline and one candidate for a numeric demand forecast. State the target, one usable predictor, and a reason the model fits.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Choose a model for the prediction task and constraints, not for its popularity.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Using visualization and numerical summaries to understand data characteristics.' WHERE id = 'a72358c1-86b4-4132-983a-badf3b599228' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Use visual and numerical summaries to find patterns, anomalies, and questions that need follow-up.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A city sees average commute time rise and wants to know whether the change affects all neighborhoods equally.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Could the citywide mean hide a small group facing a much larger delay?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>EDA inspects distributions, missingness, unusual observations, and relevant segments. It generates hypotheses; repeated searching can produce chance patterns that need separate validation.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Plot commute-time distributions by neighborhood and month, report sample counts, and check whether changes in sensor coverage could explain an apparent shift.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose two segments and one plot for the commute question. State a pattern that would prompt investigation but would not yet justify a causal claim.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Exploration makes the next question sharper; it does not by itself explain why a pattern occurred.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Data integration, data wrangling, handling missing values, and data transformation.' WHERE id = 'aef0482c-160a-41a1-b3c4-2a60defc393b' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Document source, grain, missingness, duplicates, and transformations before analysis.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A research team joins registrations to attendance records, but several participants registered twice and some attendance dates are blank.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What does one row represent in each source, and which key supports a valid join?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Data provenance records where data came from and how it was collected. Cleaning checks types, ranges, missing values, duplicates, and units while preserving an auditable raw copy.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Count registration rows per participant before joining; decide whether repeated registrations are meaningful, then compare row counts and missingness after the join.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List three validation checks for a join between people and events. What privacy principle limits which columns should be kept?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A tidy result still needs traceable decisions and a clear account of what the data represents.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Descriptive statistics, inferential statistics, probability distributions, and statistical testing.' WHERE id = '0d722361-c5fc-49de-a702-c454c0c4402f' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Use summary statistics and probability language to describe uncertainty without overstating conclusions.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A small pilot reports a higher completion rate, but only 12 people used the new process.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Could the observed difference be ordinary variation or a selection effect?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A sample statistic summarizes observed data; probability describes uncertainty under a model of outcomes. Variation, sampling method, and measurement all affect how much confidence a result deserves.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Report the counts and rates in each group, selection method, and a range of plausible differences. Avoid saying the pilot proves a broad effect.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Explain why 9 successes out of 12 does not guarantee a 75% success rate for every future user. What additional sample or design information matters?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Statistics helps calibrate claims to evidence; it does not remove uncertainty.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Asking questions, obtaining data, exploring data, modeling, deploying, and monitoring.' WHERE id = '719aeacb-b578-45d8-9049-87dd79302581' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Trace how a project revisits question, data, analysis, evaluation, communication, and action.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A prototype predicts late deliveries accurately in a notebook, but the operations team cannot use its output.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which step was missed between model evaluation and a real decision?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A lifecycle connects framing, data collection, preparation, exploration, modeling, evaluation, communication, and monitoring. Feedback can send a project back to an earlier question or assumption.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>For a late-delivery project, agree on the cutoff, inspect timestamp quality, establish a baseline, evaluate on later weeks, and give dispatchers a safe workflow for uncertain predictions.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Place a late-delivery model into lifecycle stages. Identify one point where a stakeholder decision should change the next analysis step.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Data work is iterative, and usefulness depends on how evidence reaches a decision.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Data science is an interdisciplinary field that uses scientific methods, processes, algorithms and systems to extract knowledge.' WHERE id = 'f4ea45f0-dc02-423b-b28a-668fdfb221b5' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Explain how data science combines domain questions, analysis, computation, and communication to support a decision.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A transit agency wants to reduce missed connections but has timetable, vehicle, and passenger-count data collected by different teams.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What decision must improve before choosing an algorithm?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Data science is an end-to-end process: define a question, understand and prepare data, analyze patterns, model if appropriate, evaluate, and communicate. A model is one possible tool, not the goal.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Start by defining a missed connection and who can act on the result. A clear operational summary may be more useful than a prediction model.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Write one decision-focused question for a real service. Name a stakeholder, an outcome, and a simple analysis that might answer it without ML.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A strong analysis begins with a decision and ends with evidence someone can use.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'Applications of data science in specific fields' WHERE id = 'f7a8f4e1-e0ad-4e7b-baef-838fa7ef1f3f' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Communicate domain-sensitive findings and complete an investigation with clear evidence, limitations, and recommendations.';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'Handling large-scale data processing' WHERE id = 'f69172b4-62eb-4e96-9a6b-4113eed260cd' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Recognize when data scale changes tools and architecture, and justify simpler alternatives where they are sufficient.';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'Building predictive and descriptive models' WHERE id = 'dfcbbde3-6e87-4299-aca3-5c05513f99aa' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Choose features and models only when they serve the question, then evaluate them on data that represents future use.';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'Core concepts and workflow in data science' WHERE id = 'a4397227-ba26-4d97-b626-64f41af3bed8' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Frame a useful question and prepare trustworthy evidence through provenance checks, cleaning, exploration, and statistical reasoning.';
-- UPDATE public.courses SET description = 'Design and deploy scalable applications on Amazon Web Services.' WHERE id = 'aws_architect' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'By the end, you will turn workload requirements into a secure, resilient AWS architecture proposal and explain service choices, trade-offs, failure recovery, monitoring, and cost. Basic networking concepts are helpful; no AWS account or deployment is required. Plan for about 10 hours. Your outcome is a diagram and decision record for a small scalable web application.';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Provides customers with the ability to select and download specific AWS compliance reports.' WHERE id = 'f98dbec3-d39f-431e-891e-aae57806054d' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Distinguish provider compliance evidence from the customer’s own control responsibilities and present an architecture proposal.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A startup prepares to launch a customer portal and assumes a cloud compliance report proves its application is secure.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which controls are operated by AWS, and which belong to the application team?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Compliance reports describe provider controls and attestations for defined scopes and periods. Customers remain responsible for identity, application configuration, data handling, and evidence appropriate to their own obligations.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Architecture proposal project: define user and workload requirements; diagram DNS, edge, network, compute, database, and storage flow; document IAM and encryption; explain scaling, availability, backup/restore, monitoring, cost assumptions, and trade-offs.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Present the proposal, one failure scenario and recovery path, security boundaries, estimated cost drivers, evidence source, unresolved risks, and the next validation step. Do not claim a cloud report certifies the application.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Architecture is a set of justified trade-offs and owned controls. No learner environment or cloud deployment is created by this lesson.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Aggregates, organizes, and prioritizes security alerts from multiple AWS services.' WHERE id = 'd63a725e-7d37-4d87-9b07-6c255632431f' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Use aggregated security findings to prioritize owned risks without treating a dashboard as a remediation plan.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A team receives hundreds of alerts from several accounts and cannot tell which issue could expose customer data.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What context is needed to prioritize by impact and owner?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A security findings service aggregates and prioritizes signals from integrated controls. Teams must triage duplicates, assign ownership, validate context, and track remediation.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Group related public-storage findings, confirm whether the objects contain sensitive data, assign an accountable owner, and record a target date and verification evidence.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Rank three fictional findings by likelihood, impact, and exposure. What additional evidence could change their order?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Security findings become useful when they lead to verified fixes and accountable follow-up.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Lets you easily provision, manage, and deploy public and private SSL/TLS certificates.' WHERE id = 'aecc5d5b-8819-4ee9-b453-3b2365b6ab78' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Plan certificate lifecycle and TLS termination for a public application endpoint.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A site certificate expires during a campaign and visitors see browser warnings.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which component owns renewal and which endpoint presents the certificate?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Certificate management provisions and renews certificates for supported AWS integrations. The architecture must still route the correct hostname, protect private keys, and validate the TLS configuration.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Attach a managed certificate to the public load balancer or CDN, automate DNS validation, and monitor domain ownership and renewal status.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Trace a browser HTTPS request to the certificate-bearing endpoint. What check confirms the name and renewal setup are correct?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Automated certificate renewal reduces operational risk when domain and endpoint configuration are correct.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Managed service that makes it easy for you to create and control encryption keys.' WHERE id = 'a9198e1f-676a-44ef-8d87-c76eb8809d77' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Explain how managed encryption keys support data protection and key-access separation.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A finance team encrypts stored reports but allows every application role to decrypt every file.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Does encryption help if the same identities can freely obtain plaintext?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>KMS manages cryptographic keys and policy-controlled operations. Encryption at rest requires decisions about key access, rotation, audit, and recovery; it does not replace application authorization.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Separate a data-processing role’s decrypt permission from a reporting role’s use of approved outputs, and monitor key-use events.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Draw who can request encryption and decryption for a private report bucket. Identify one role that should not decrypt the data.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Key policy and workload identity determine who can turn protected data back into readable data.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Web service that helps you securely control access to AWS resources for your users.' WHERE id = 'c40e84cd-4bd7-42a5-abd6-f375ae1edb6c' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Apply least privilege to a workload role and distinguish user identity from resource permission.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A nightly report job only needs to read one bucket, but it currently uses an administrator key.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What is the smallest permission that lets the job finish?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>IAM policies grant or deny actions on resources under conditions. Workloads should use roles and short-lived credentials where possible; broad user keys create unnecessary exposure.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Give a report role read access to a specific input prefix and write access only to its output location. Review access logs and rotate any exposed credential.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Rewrite a broad “all actions on all resources” request as two narrow permissions for a fictional job.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Identity and resource permissions are architecture controls that should match one named task.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Uses the AWS global network to route traffic via optimal endpoints.' WHERE id = '6c79c871-5dba-4f55-a576-c8b29ac2122d' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Compare global traffic acceleration with content caching and regional application design.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A global API serves dynamic requests from several regions, while some static images already use a CDN.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would caching the API response be safe, and is latency caused by routing or origin processing?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Global Accelerator uses AWS edge network entry points to route traffic toward configured endpoints. It does not cache application content or fix slow backend code.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Use an accelerator when global network routing and endpoint failover meet a measured need; use a CDN for cacheable objects and profile server work separately.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose between CDN, accelerator, or neither for static images, user-specific API data, and a single-region local service. State the requirement behind each choice.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Edge services solve different problems; measure the bottleneck before adding one.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Makes it easier to build a hybrid environment.' WHERE id = '363d9ed1-826c-4af1-bfaf-efa70c3c6977' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Decide whether dedicated connectivity is justified by predictable traffic, latency, or data-transfer requirements.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A company sends large data sets between its office network and cloud environment every day.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would a dedicated circuit solve an application availability problem by itself?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Direct Connect provides a dedicated network path to AWS. It needs provider coordination, routing design, and a separate resilience plan; it is not automatically encrypted end to end.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Use private connectivity for steady hybrid transfer if the cost and lead time fit, and add independent connectivity or VPN recovery for outages.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List the reason to use dedicated connectivity, a failure scenario, and a separate recovery path.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A private path changes connectivity characteristics but does not replace encryption or failover planning.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Application Load Balancer, Network Load Balancer, and Classic Load Balancer.' WHERE id = 'a87f7b69-cdcd-4ae9-919d-c12bdfc62e05' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose a load balancer type from protocol, traffic, health, and routing requirements.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A web application has two instances, but traffic still goes to one even after it stops responding.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What signal tells the traffic layer that an instance is ready and healthy?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A load balancer distributes requests across targets and performs health checks. Application and network load balancers serve different protocol and routing needs; health checks must test meaningful readiness.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Route HTTPS requests by host or path to healthy application targets, keep instances private, and confirm a failed target is removed without dropping all traffic.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Pick a load-balancing behavior for a web API and a non-HTTP TCP service. What should the health check test for each?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Traffic distribution improves resilience when health signals and target capacity are sound.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Reliable and cost-effective way to route users to Internet Applications.' WHERE id = 'e85c8917-38d3-464d-8048-81a409b78734' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Explain how DNS records direct a name to an endpoint and why a DNS change is not an instant failover guarantee.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A team changes its application hosting and expects users to reach the new target immediately.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What cached answer might a resolver still be using?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>DNS maps names to records with a time-to-live. Route policies can support routing decisions, but client and resolver caching plus health-check timing affect failover.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Lower a record’s TTL before a planned migration, validate the new destination, then preserve the old endpoint through the transition window.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Create a migration sequence for a hostname. What must be verified before changing the record and after the TTL period?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>DNS is part of traffic management; plan for caching and observation.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Works with other AWS services to deliver data, videos, applications, and APIs globally.' WHERE id = '50ffe82f-fb02-42d8-9209-b40f3de9ecc4' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Use edge delivery to improve access to cacheable content while planning invalidation and origin protection.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>Visitors in several countries download the same product images from one origin location.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which content can be cached safely, and what must remain private or current?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A content delivery network caches eligible responses near users. Cache keys, time-to-live, origin access, and invalidation determine correctness and exposure.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Cache versioned public assets for a long period; use private distribution controls for protected files and avoid caching user-specific account responses publicly.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Classify product images, account pages, and a versioned stylesheet as public-cacheable, private, or short-lived. Explain the freshness tradeoff.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Caching improves latency only when the content and access policy are safe to reuse.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'MySQL- and PostgreSQL-compatible relational database engine.' WHERE id = '55b9ba12-135a-45ef-86e1-c6c422ceff7d' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Compare a managed compatible relational engine with a standard database option against workload and operational requirements.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A service needs relational transactions and expects seasonal growth but has no database operations team.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which requirement is about compatibility, and which is about availability or scale?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Aurora provides managed relational engines compatible with MySQL or PostgreSQL interfaces. Compatibility does not mean identical behavior, features, cost, or migration effort.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Prototype critical queries and drivers, test failover and restore time, and compare projected usage and storage costs before a move.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List two compatibility checks and one cost question before selecting Aurora for an existing application.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A service label is not an architecture decision; validate the exact workload and operating model.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Facilitates migration of databases to AWS with minimal downtime.' WHERE id = '6fa09853-3f5a-4f7d-9889-189bb4d55c1a' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Plan a database migration around compatibility, data validation, downtime, rollback, and ownership.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>An online store must move an older order database while keeping new purchases available.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>How will the team know every record and update arrived correctly before switching users?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A migration service can replicate and transform data, but it does not remove schema differences, application compatibility checks, or cutover planning.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Inventory schemas, run a test migration, compare counts and sampled checksums, capture ongoing changes, rehearse cutover, and retain a rollback window.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Write a go/no-go checklist with one data-validation condition, one downtime limit, and one rollback trigger.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A migration is complete only when the data and application behavior are verified.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud.' WHERE id = '9b96ba17-433e-4747-9990-66215f96b09f' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Separate analytical workloads from transactional operations and select a warehouse only when the reporting need warrants it.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>Analysts run large historical sales summaries that slow down the database used for checkout.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Should long scans compete with customer transactions on the same workload?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A data warehouse is optimized for analytical queries over large datasets. Redshift can support these workloads, but ingestion, retention, concurrency, and cost need a plan.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Copy curated daily sales facts into an analytical store, restrict sensitive columns, and refresh on a schedule suited to reporting needs.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Decide whether a small business with weekly summaries needs a warehouse yet. What volume or query pattern would justify the added service?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A scalable warehouse is useful when the analysis workload and operating cost justify it.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Key-value and document database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance.' WHERE id = '0022df88-3e77-440d-b202-732b9b5e679e' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Design a key-value access pattern before choosing a NoSQL table for predictable high-scale lookups.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A game service retrieves each player’s current session by player ID and updates a small set of session fields.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Does the product need flexible joins, or a very fast lookup by known key?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>DynamoDB is a managed key-value and document database. Its partition and sort keys should be designed from access patterns; ad hoc relational joins are not its strength.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>A table keyed by player ID can serve the session read directly. Add a secondary index only for a real query and consider hot keys and item-size limits.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List the reads and writes for a session service, then propose a primary key. Which new query might require a different index or data model?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>NoSQL performance follows from an intentional data model and known access patterns.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Understanding when to use each storage type based on access patterns and durability requirements.' WHERE id = '73451f03-204f-4ed2-9416-d283e4aa521c' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Compare block, object, and shared file storage by access pattern, performance, and operating need.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A workload needs boot volumes, user-uploaded documents, and shared files used by several application instances.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would the same storage interface suit all three jobs?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Block storage presents volumes to compute; object storage exposes objects through APIs; file storage provides shared directories. Durability, latency, concurrency, and cost differ.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Use a boot volume for an instance, an object bucket for uploads, and shared file storage only if several instances require filesystem semantics.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Match each storage need to a type and state one operational or cost trade-off for your choice.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Select storage from the way the application reads and writes data.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Launch AWS resources into a virtual network that you''ve defined.' WHERE id = 'e922f1d2-729b-4ae7-8e07-64d551a150ea' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Draw a VPC path that separates public entry points from private application and data resources.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A prototype places the web server and database on public addresses to make setup easy.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which components need inbound internet traffic, and which should only receive internal requests?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A VPC defines a virtual network with subnets, routes, and security controls. Public reachability depends on routing and gateways, not merely the subnet name.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Route internet traffic to a public load balancer; keep app instances and database in private subnets; permit only the required app-to-database port.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Sketch public and private subnets plus one request flow. Mark where a route, security group, and database access rule each apply.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Network isolation is a set of explicit paths and permissions, not a label alone.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Easy setup, operation, and scaling of a relational database.' WHERE id = 'abf128ad-c846-4775-a16b-d6e9ddd54778' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose a managed relational database for structured relationships and define backups, availability, and connection limits.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>Orders, customers, and payments must remain consistent when checkout writes several related records.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would separate uncoordinated files preserve these relationships reliably?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>RDS manages relational database infrastructure and common operational tasks. The workload still needs schema design, access control, capacity planning, backups, and tested recovery.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Use a private database subnet, narrowly scoped application access, automated backups, and connection pooling appropriate to expected concurrency.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List the recovery point and recovery time a checkout system needs. Which backup and failover decisions must be tested rather than assumed?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Managed operations reduce some work, but data consistency and recovery remain architecture responsibilities.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Object storage built to store and retrieve any amount of data from anywhere.' WHERE id = 'bbcb6628-544b-43e0-a5de-92c4820b8830' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Use object storage for durable, access-controlled objects and choose lifecycle behavior from retrieval needs.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A photo service stores original uploads, thumbnails, and temporary exports with different access patterns.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Do all objects need the same retention, public access, and retrieval speed?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>S3 stores objects in buckets with policies, versioning, encryption options, and lifecycle transitions. It is not a mounted relational database; design access and recovery explicitly.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Keep original uploads private, serve authorized content through an application or controlled distribution path, and expire temporary exports after their business need ends.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Classify three example files by access, retention, and recovery requirement. What bucket policy mistake could expose all customer images?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Object durability does not automatically provide the right access policy or recovery plan.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Resizable compute capacity in the cloud with virtual servers.' WHERE id = 'ae98043a-8e30-46d9-a2d4-1f340a2b458e' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose virtual compute when control over the operating environment is worth its management work.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A team runs a specialized background processor that needs a custom runtime not supported by its current managed platform.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What operational tasks return to the team when it manages a virtual machine?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>EC2 provides virtual instances with configurable compute, storage, and networking. The customer manages the guest operating system, patches, capacity settings, and application while AWS operates the underlying infrastructure.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Place private application instances behind a load balancer, use an instance role instead of static keys, and define scaling and patch ownership.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Compare EC2 with a managed application service for a small API. Name one control EC2 adds and one maintenance responsibility it creates.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Compute flexibility is valuable when the workload needs it and an owner can maintain it.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Amazon Web Services (AWS) is the world''s most comprehensive and broadly adopted cloud platform.' WHERE id = '38d8e8dd-e9a7-4484-876b-f8283416e113' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Convert a workload brief into requirements before selecting cloud services.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A local retailer plans to launch a product website, expects uncertain traffic, and must protect customer orders.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What availability, data, latency, recovery, and budget needs would change the design?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Cloud architecture maps requirements to components and their interactions. AWS operates across regions and availability zones, but service choice, configuration, and shared-responsibility controls remain design decisions.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Write measurable targets such as expected peak requests, acceptable recovery time, data retention, and monthly budget before drawing the first service box.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Create five questions for a product owner that would guide a first architecture. Which answer is a constraint rather than a preference?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Requirements make service selection explainable and testable.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'Protecting AWS resources and data' WHERE id = '6913e95a-e890-498b-a1bd-115c5dca237e' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Apply least privilege, encryption, certificate management, security findings, and a reviewable architecture proposal.';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'AWS services for networking and content distribution' WHERE id = '55f6e56c-928e-4254-afd3-db8d567d63cb' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Design name resolution, traffic distribution, private connectivity, and edge delivery around latency and failure needs.';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'AWS services for data storage and management' WHERE id = '4ce26689-9ca4-4d27-905c-8fc1972322ae' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Match object, relational, key-value, warehouse, and migration services to data shape and access patterns.';
-- UPDATE public.modules SET description = 'Fundamental AWS services for building applications' WHERE id = '62de0492-bbae-46bb-b055-de48cd329615' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Translate workload requirements into network, compute, and data choices with explicit access and availability boundaries.';
-- UPDATE public.courses SET description = 'Deep dive into neural networks, NLP, and computer vision.' WHERE id = 'advanced_ml' AND description IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'By the end, you will design a reproducible ML experiment, choose a model and features for a defined task, evaluate it on suitable data, interpret errors, and propose a safe production plan. Prior ML and basic Python are recommended; plan for about 12 hours. Your outcome is an evaluated, documented model proposal with limitations and next experiments.';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Data ingestion, preprocessing, training, evaluation, deployment, and monitoring.' WHERE id = '956c11d3-a7d1-4dfd-94e2-3cfec094b72b' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Assemble a reproducible model workflow and present evidence, limitations, and a next experiment.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A small operations team wants to forecast equipment service needs from historical sensor summaries before planning inspections.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What decision will a forecast change, what data exists at prediction time, and how will success be measured?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Project: define the decision and intended user; inspect a documented dataset; create a time-aware split; establish a baseline; engineer and version features; compare a justified model; evaluate suitable metrics and slices; interpret errors; document a bounded serving and monitoring plan.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>For rare failures, report precision-recall behavior and the workload implied by alerts. Keep an untouched future period for final evaluation and never treat a public sample as production evidence.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Present the question, data provenance, feature choices, baseline/model comparison, evaluation, interpretation, failure limits, responsible-use boundary, and next experiment. Reproduce the result from recorded code and configuration.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A model is ready for review when its evidence can be reproduced and its limits guide the next decision. This platform does not store learner project submissions.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Identifying and reducing unfair bias in machine learning models.' WHERE id = 'bf15613f-e7cc-4a11-86cb-05724928cb43' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Identify a consequential failure and choose evaluation slices and mitigations that fit the actual decision.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A hiring support model ranks applications, and review shows different error rates across job families.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which groups and outcomes matter, and what decision is the model permitted to influence?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Fairness is context-dependent. Inspect data coverage, label quality, error rates by relevant groups, and downstream effects. Interpretation tools such as permutation importance explain model behavior only under assumptions; correlated features and subgroup shifts can mislead.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Compare false-negative rates by relevant population, then use a held-out representative set to inspect important features and example-level errors. Ask accountable stakeholders whether the proposed use is appropriate.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Define an audit slice, one harmful error, one interpretation check and its limitation, a human-review safeguard, and evidence that would cause the team to pause deployment.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Responsible evaluation includes who bears errors and whether the use should proceed at all.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Statistical methods for comparing two versions to determine which performs better.' WHERE id = '640da9d3-3444-4fd3-a867-5e9bf6e72c57' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Plan a controlled experiment with a primary metric, guardrails, randomization unit, and stopping rule.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A marketplace wants to know whether a new ranking model improves useful clicks without reducing successful purchases.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Could a higher click rate be a bad outcome if purchases fall or one group sees worse results?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A/B testing compares assigned variants under an experiment design. Choose a unit that avoids contamination, define one primary outcome and guardrails, and account for duration and repeated peeking.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Randomize by user when ranking exposures repeat; track purchase completion and latency as guardrails. Check assignment balance before interpreting the outcome.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Write a primary metric, one guardrail, a randomization unit, and a condition for stopping the experiment safely.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>An experiment answers a narrow causal question only when its assignment and measurements support that claim.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Detecting concept drift, monitoring prediction distributions, and setting up alerts.' WHERE id = '292c0f46-a0fa-4642-ae4f-77efca49fbf6' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Separate service health, data drift, and model quality monitoring and define a response owner.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A model’s API remains fast, but fewer customers accept its recommendations after a pricing policy changes.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would uptime alone reveal that the model no longer helps?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Monitor latency and errors, input distributions, prediction patterns, and delayed ground-truth outcomes. Drift is a signal to investigate, not proof that retraining is needed.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Track a privacy-conscious feature summary and later acceptance by relevant segment. Set a review threshold, owner, and rollback option before release.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>For a delayed-label task, name one immediate signal and one later quality measure. What action follows an alert, and who decides?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Monitoring is useful when signals have context, ownership, and a safe response.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'REST APIs, gRPC, and batch prediction systems for serving predictions.' WHERE id = '9b5709aa-e3de-461b-a81e-cbecc4672a33' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose online or batch inference and define a versioned prediction contract with safe input and failure behavior.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A retailer needs product recommendations on page load, while nightly inventory risk forecasts can wait until morning.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Do both requests need a synchronous API with the same latency and availability target?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Online serving returns a prediction during a request; batch inference processes many records on a schedule. Version features and model artifacts, validate inputs, and define fallback behavior.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Serve recommendations with a strict timeout and a cached or popularity fallback; run overnight forecasts as a traceable batch with row counts and error summaries.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose serving style for a checkout fraud signal and a monthly demand report. State a latency target, failure response, and artifact version to record.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Production design begins with the user-facing contract, not the choice of serving framework.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'From self-driving cars to cancer detection in radiological images.' WHERE id = 'e6345d01-c6e7-479f-b65c-f60f11e6a43f' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Assess high-impact vision applications through error consequences, oversight, and limits on intended use.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A hospital evaluates an imaging model that flags possible findings for clinician review.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Which error is more harmful in this workflow, and who remains responsible for a decision?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>High-impact models need representative evaluation, calibrated communication, subgroup analysis, human oversight, and a clear operational boundary. Test performance does not establish clinical safety or driving readiness.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Report sensitivity and false-negative patterns across relevant equipment and populations, route uncertain cases to qualified review, and monitor changes in acquisition conditions.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Write a deployment boundary for a fictional imaging aid. Name the responsible professional, a failure mode, and evidence required before expanding use.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A model score supports a bounded workflow; it does not transfer accountability from people.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Spatial and temporal analysis of video sequences for activity recognition.' WHERE id = '571395c7-0b8a-4697-80a3-2409de328d18' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Explain why video evaluation must preserve temporal order and distinguish object tracking from single-frame detection.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A transit team estimates passenger flow from station video, but repeated frames from one event are highly similar.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What would happen if neighboring frames were split randomly between training and testing?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Tracking links detections across frames; temporal models use order and motion. Split evaluation by recording session or location, protect privacy, and avoid retaining identifiable footage unnecessarily.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Evaluate on later sessions from a different camera angle, report tracking continuity and missed counts, and aggregate or blur data according to the approved purpose.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Propose a split that prevents near-duplicate frames crossing partitions. State one privacy safeguard and one failure measure.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Video adds temporal leakage and privacy risks; handle both as core design constraints.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Semantic, instance, and panoptic segmentation techniques.' WHERE id = 'f357ce62-e33c-468a-8146-bfa4e482b36f' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose semantic or instance segmentation based on whether separate object identities matter.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A landscaping team estimates plant-covered area in plots where several plants overlap.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Does the task need a mask for each individual plant, or only a plant-versus-background map?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Semantic segmentation labels each pixel by class; instance segmentation separates individual objects. Annotation boundaries, class imbalance, and evaluation overlap metrics affect interpretation.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>For total vegetation area, semantic masks may be sufficient; for counting overlapping plants, instance masks may be needed. Compare predictions with carefully reviewed annotations.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose a segmentation type for area estimation and for counting separate objects. What annotation burden does each choice create?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Let the decision determine the label structure and evaluation, rather than choosing the most complex model.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Traditional computer vision approaches and deep learning-based methods.' WHERE id = '3c443a45-8d99-490e-95bb-88b27006b75a' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Distinguish image classification from object detection and evaluate localization as well as class correctness.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A warehouse wants to count damaged packages in a scene containing several items.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Is one label for the whole image enough to identify each package and its location?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Classification assigns labels to an image; detection predicts boxes and classes for multiple objects. Precision-recall tradeoffs and intersection-over-union thresholds affect detection scores.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Annotate a small, consented set with boxes, define how overlapping items are labeled, and review misses by object size and lighting.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose a metric and review slice for tiny packages at the edge of a frame. What operational action should occur when confidence is low?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Evaluate whether the system finds the objects people need, not only whether its class label is plausible.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Filters, transformations, frequency domain processing, and morphological operations.' WHERE id = 'c6822e43-0165-4f3e-bc45-5ed57a3ef791' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Inspect image size, color, orientation, and transformation choices before training a vision model.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A quality team combines photographs from two phone models; one saves rotated images and the other uses a different color profile.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Could preprocessing differences be mistaken for the target label?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Images are arrays of pixel values. Resizing, normalization, color conversion, and augmentation change the input distribution; fit choices using training data and preserve labels through transformations.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Check a grid of representative images and metadata before resizing. Apply only label-preserving augmentation such as a modest crop when object orientation is irrelevant.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List an image check for dimensions, orientation, and label validity. Name an augmentation that would invalidate a “left-facing” label.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Visualize transformed samples; never assume a preprocessing step preserves the task.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Self-attention mechanism, encoder-decoder structure, and bidirectional encoder representations.' WHERE id = '5b0d1e3a-7682-4be7-bc34-1b5ddf9dacd9' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Describe encoder-style contextual representations and choose a fine-tuning task with an appropriate split.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A research team labels short abstracts by topic and wants a model that uses the surrounding words to resolve ambiguous terms.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Why might the same word carry different meaning in different sentences?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A transformer encoder builds contextual token representations using self-attention. BERT-style models are pretrained with language objectives and can be fine-tuned for classification or token labeling.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Fine-tune a small classifier on labeled abstracts, keep documents from the same source together across splits, and compare with a TF-IDF baseline.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Describe one reason a random sentence split could overstate performance if abstracts from one report appear in both train and test sets.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Contextual models are powerful, but data boundaries and baselines still determine whether results are credible.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'LSTM, GRU, and attention mechanisms for processing text sequences.' WHERE id = '7bf81f87-6e5f-4ce0-a9f7-24f0a15ec13d' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Compare recurrent context with attention-based context when selecting a sequence model.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A legal aid service summarizes long forms, where an important date near the beginning may relate to a decision near the end.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>How might direct attention between distant tokens help, and what resource cost does it introduce?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>RNNs carry state sequentially; transformers use attention to relate tokens more directly and parallelize training. Attention cost and context limits grow with sequence length, and neither model guarantees faithful reasoning.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>For short sensor streams, a compact recurrent model may fit latency constraints; for long text, a transformer may capture context but require truncation or chunking.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose a model family for short device readings and for long documents. State a quality, latency, or context-length test for each choice.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Model selection balances task behavior, compute, context, and operational constraints.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Word2Vec, GloVe, and FastText for capturing semantic relationships between words.' WHERE id = 'ccb53ed7-e0bc-4e27-bfca-1bde22846b20' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Explain what an embedding encodes and why similarity in vector space is not the same as truth or fairness.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A catalog search wants related terms such as “jacket” and “coat” to retrieve nearby products.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What does a nearby vector suggest, and what does it not prove about a result?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>An embedding maps tokens or text into numeric vectors learned from context or another objective. Similarity reflects training data and objective; it can preserve stereotypes and miss domain-specific meanings.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Compare nearest neighbors for a domain term before and after adapting representations. Review examples across groups and treat similarity as a retrieval clue, not a factual judgment.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose a small set of terms for a nearest-neighbor audit. What biased association, ambiguity, or out-of-domain behavior will you look for?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Representations inherit assumptions from data and need task-specific review.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition, and sentiment analysis.' WHERE id = '596a4dfa-1708-48ec-9a22-ecff41d8101c' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Select a text task and baseline technique, then inspect errors before escalating model complexity.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A service desk routes short requests to billing, account access, or technical support.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Could a transparent bag-of-words model solve enough of the task before adding a large language model?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Sparse term features with logistic regression or a linear SVM often provide fast, inspectable baselines. For imbalanced labels, compare class weights or carefully sampled training data and evaluate with per-class precision, recall, and a precision-recall curve.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Train a TF-IDF plus linear classifier inside cross-validation, keep resampling within each training fold, then inspect de-identified false routes for missing vocabulary or ambiguous labels.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>For rare account-access requests, which metric reveals missed cases better than overall accuracy? Compare class weighting with a human fallback and state the workload trade-off.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A modest model with clear errors can be a better engineering choice than an opaque, unnecessary one.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Tokenization, stemming, lemmatization, and vectorization techniques like TF-IDF and word embeddings.' WHERE id = 'b60f2e60-4ae9-4bf1-a0aa-50151e38d7ec' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose a text representation that preserves distinctions needed by the task and avoids fitting preprocessing on test data.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A support team classifies short messages, but product names and misspellings carry useful signals.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would stemming every word improve the result, or could it erase a distinction the classifier needs?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Tokenization turns text into units; TF-IDF weights terms by document frequency; embeddings represent tokens as learned vectors. Fit vocabulary and transformations on training data only.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Build a TF-IDF pipeline inside cross-validation so vocabulary is learned per training fold. Compare lowercasing and token choices with a held-out set.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Name one tokenization decision for product codes and one leakage risk if the full corpus is used to fit vocabulary before splitting.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Text preparation is part of the model and must be evaluated without peeking at held-out examples.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Using models trained on large datasets as starting points for new tasks.' WHERE id = 'ece17f4d-6418-493a-9b2b-16a5b8f06305' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Decide whether a pretrained model is a good starting point and plan a careful fine-tuning comparison.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A small conservation group has only a few hundred labeled camera-trap images of local wildlife.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What useful visual patterns might a model have learned from a larger image collection, and what might not transfer?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Transfer learning reuses parameters learned on another dataset. Freeze earlier layers or fine-tune selected layers, compare with a simple baseline, and guard against duplicate images across splits.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Resize images consistently, keep one site or time period for evaluation, and compare a frozen-feature classifier with a lightly fine-tuned model.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>List two reasons a pretrained model may fail on night-vision images. What small experiment would test whether fine-tuning helps?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Pretraining can reduce data needs, but domain mismatch and split leakage can create false confidence.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Vanishing/exploding gradients, overfitting, regularization techniques, and batch normalization.' WHERE id = 'f9963def-73e6-4720-9147-2b2f37cc1114' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Diagnose underfitting, overfitting, and unstable training with learning curves and controlled changes.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A team reports near-perfect training accuracy, while performance falls sharply on a later month of customer records.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Does the model generalize, or has it memorized quirks in the training examples?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>Overfitting occurs when a model captures training-specific detail that does not transfer. Weight decay, dropout, early stopping, and smaller models can help. Tune learning rate or regularization on validation data, not on the final test set.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>If training loss keeps falling while validation loss rises, stop at the best validation checkpoint. Run a small, recorded hyperparameter search that changes one planned setting at a time.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Given improving training and worsening validation curves, propose one intervention and one diagnostic check. State the tuning range, selection metric, and why the test set must remain untouched.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Use validation behavior to guide controlled experiments; never tune repeatedly against the final test set.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'LSTM and GRU architectures for handling temporal dependencies in data.' WHERE id = '8e5864ac-fe6a-412b-ba7d-9f951830031c' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Identify when sequence order matters and explain the hidden state used by a recurrent model.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A factory predicts whether a machine needs inspection from the last hour of vibration readings.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Would an unordered average preserve a short spike that occurred just before a fault?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>An RNN processes a sequence one step at a time and carries a state forward. LSTM and GRU gates help preserve useful signals across longer sequences, but training can be slower and long-range context remains difficult.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>Keep readings in timestamp order, define the forecast horizon, and split by machine or time so adjacent windows do not leak across evaluation sets.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Choose one sequence length and prediction horizon for the factory task. What failure would result from randomly splitting overlapping windows?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>Sequence models depend on ordering and evaluation that respects time and entity boundaries.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Architecture, convolutional layers, pooling layers, and applications in computer vision.' WHERE id = 'f5f6eafb-aea1-4b08-bf9e-27668b8638a2' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Explain how local filters and shared weights make convolutional networks useful for image patterns.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A recycling center wants to sort photographs of bottles, cans, and paper, even when an item appears at different image locations.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>Why might a model that learns local shape patterns reuse the same detector across an image?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A convolution applies learned filters across nearby pixels, sharing weights across positions. Pooling or strided layers reduce spatial size. These choices encode useful assumptions but do not guarantee robustness to lighting or viewpoint.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>A small CNN can detect edges and textures before combining them into class evidence. Keep an untouched test set from a later collection day to check changing camera conditions.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Sketch what a small receptive field can notice and name two image changes that might still confuse the classifier.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>CNN structure matches spatial data, while performance still depends on representative images and honest testing.</p></section>';
-- UPDATE public.lessons SET content = 'Multilayer perceptrons, activation functions, loss functions, and gradient descent optimization.' WHERE id = 'b930d261-4902-413a-b2ab-bc5a03a20680' AND content IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '<section class="lesson-section"><h3>Learning Objective</h3><p>Choose a neural baseline and describe the inputs, target, loss, and comparison needed for a defensible experiment.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Story</h3><p>A city estimates next-day electricity demand. The data team has many weather, calendar, and meter variables, but no clear benchmark.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Discovery</h3><p>What simple approach should a neural model beat, and which information would not exist at prediction time?</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Concept</h3><p>A neural network learns adjustable weights that transform features into predictions. Feature choices encode domain assumptions; a loss measures training error. Compare model capacity, latency, and a simple baseline using a split that matches future use.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Example</h3><p>For next-day demand, compare a neural model with yesterday’s demand and a seasonal baseline. Encode calendar effects only from information known at forecast time; exclude tomorrow’s actual temperature.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Challenge</h3><p>Write the prediction time, target, two available features, one likely leakage feature, and a baseline you would report beside a neural result.</p></section>
-- <section class="lesson-section"><h3>Takeaway</h3><p>A sophisticated architecture is useful only when its evaluation answers a real decision better than a credible baseline.</p></section>';
